Title: The Korean War: Was it Really Initiated by South Korea?

The Korean War, also known as the Korean Conflict, broke out in 1950-53, lasting for three years until an armistice was finally called in 1953. Armistice. The war was fought between the Republic of Korea, supported by the United States and the United Nations, and the Democratic People's Republic of Korea, supported by China and the Soviet Union. The conflict started when the South Korean government, led by Syngman Rhee, made a move to unify the peninsula under its control, with the help of the US. The North, led by Kim Il Sung, responded by invading the South.

However, many South Korean experts argue that the Korean War was solely initiated by South Korea, and the North's involvement was purely defensive. They point to several factors that led to this conclusion.

